import Foundation

enum SuccessArchitectData360QuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A retail customer ingests order data via a CRM Data Stream from Salesforce and a nightly file via the Ingestion API for a legacy POS system. The architect needs the POS file data to land as a queryable Data Model Object with minimal manual field mapping effort, and it must refresh incrementally without re-processing the full file each night. Which approach best satisfies both requirements?", options: [("A", "Ingest to a Data Lake Object, map it to a custom DMO, and configure the data stream refresh mode as Incremental Refresh with a designated incremental field."), ("B", "Use a Batch Data Transform to load the file directly into the Individual DMO, bypassing DLO staging."), ("C", "Create the data stream as Full Refresh only, since Ingestion API sources don't support incremental refresh."), ("D", "Configure the Ingestion API stream to write directly to a DMO and set the refresh mode to Full Refresh.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Ingestion API sources always land first as a Data Lake Object; mapping the DLO to a DMO and setting Incremental Refresh with an incremental field lets only new/changed records process each run, satisfying both the queryability and incremental-load requirements.\n\n* B fails because Batch Transforms operate on already-ingested DLOs/DMOs, not raw ingestion files, and writing directly into the standard Individual DMO bypasses required identity/mapping steps.  \n* C fails on a factual constraint: Ingestion API streams do support Incremental Refresh when an incremental field is designated.  \n* D fails because Ingestion API streams cannot write directly to a DMO — they must stage as a DLO first, and Full Refresh reprocesses the entire file every run."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "A subscription business wants a Calculated Insight that recalculates customer lifetime value daily and joins data from two DMOs that are each refreshed on different schedules (one hourly, one daily). Support cases show CI failures occur intermittently right after the hourly DMO's refresh window. What is the most likely architectural cause, and what should the architect change?", options: [("A", "The CI formula exceeds the maximum allowed join complexity; simplify to a single-source CI."), ("B", "The CI is running against a DMO whose data stream refresh hasn't completed yet; sequence the CI's calculation schedule to run after the source stream refresh."), ("C", "Calculated Insights can't reference DMOs with different refresh cadences; consolidate to one cadence first."), ("D", "Switch the CI from batch to a Data Action so it recalculates in real time on every record change.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Calculated Insights read from DMOs at run time; if a source DMO is actively refreshing, the CI can pick up a partial or in-error state. Scheduling the CI after both underlying refresh windows finish (with buffer) avoids the race condition.\n\n* A misdiagnoses a timing issue as a complexity limit; join complexity isn't described as exceeded here.  \n* C is a fabricated constraint — DMOs with different refresh cadences can coexist as CI sources; that's a normal, supported pattern.  \n* D confuses Data Actions (real-time triggered actions) with the batch calculation engine that computes CIs; it doesn't address the timing conflict."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "A healthcare provider's Identity Resolution ruleset unifies Individuals using Email and Phone match rules with \"OR\" logic. After go-live, unrelated patients sharing a household land-office phone number are being merged into a single unified Individual, creating a compliance risk. Which change best resolves this while preserving legitimate matches on email?", options: [("A", "Remove the Phone match rule entirely and rely on Email matching only."), ("B", "Change the ruleset logic from OR to AND across all match rules."), ("C", "Keep the OR-based ruleset but add a data-quality prerequisite that normalizes phone formats before matching runs, and review outlier phone values feeding the rule."), ("D", "Switch Identity Resolution to run on a nightly batch schedule instead of triggering after each stream refresh.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Adding qualifying criteria to the Phone rule preserves its value for genuine matches while preventing a single shared attribute (a household phone) from unifying unrelated individuals — this is standard match-rule design for weak identifiers.\n\n* A over-corrects and loses valid matches where phone-based unification is legitimate and email is unavailable.  \n* B breaks matching for individuals who share only one of the two identifiers, since AND requires every rule to match simultaneously.  \n* D addresses scheduling, not the root cause — a data-quality issue in the phone field — and doesn't reduce false-positive merges."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "An architect configures a reconciliation rule strategy for a unified Individual profile where multiple source systems (CRM, e-commerce, call center) provide conflicting values for the same attribute (e.g., preferred email). The customer wants the call center system's value to always win for phone-related fields, but the e-commerce system's value to win for email fields. Which approach correctly satisfies this?", options: [("A", "Reconciliation rules apply uniformly to the entire Individual object; per-field source prioritization is not supported, so the customer must pick one authoritative source system overall."), ("B", "Configure a single reconciliation rule using \"Most Recently Updated\" for every attribute, since that always reflects the freshest source regardless of field."), ("C", "Build one reconciliation ruleset per attribute or attribute group, each specifying source priority order (e.g., CRM first for contact details, e-commerce first for loyalty tier), so each field independently resolves from its preferred source."), ("D", "Use Data Actions to overwrite the Individual DMO fields nightly based on a custom Apex priority list, since reconciliation rules cannot be scoped by field.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Data 360 supports configuring reconciliation rules at the attribute (or attribute-group) level, each with its own source-priority ordering — this lets different fields defer to different \"winning\" source systems, exactly matching the customer's requirement.\n\n* A is a fabricated platform limitation — per-field/attribute-group reconciliation rules with distinct source priority are a supported, standard capability.  \n* B ignores that \"Most Recently Updated\" is only one of several reconciliation strategies, and using it universally can pull stale or wrong data for fields where source system matters more than recency.  \n* C is the correct approach, not a wrong answer here.\n\n---"),
        Question(id: "5", question: "A customer disables several fields on a CRM Data Stream, and the next refresh throws a generic Gack error instead of a clear message. Investigation reveals the mapping still references a field that no longer exists on the source object. What should the architect recommend as the standing operational practice to prevent this class of failure?", options: [("A", "Before deactivating or deleting any source field referenced in an active stream mapping, first remove or remap that field in the Data Stream mapping UI so the mapping no longer references it."), ("B", "Deactivate the field immediately — Data 360 automatically detects and removes orphaned mappings on the next scheduled refresh."), ("C", "Deactivating a field only affects reporting; it has no effect on Data Stream refreshes since mappings are cached at creation time."), ("D", "Switch the affected stream to Full Refresh mode first, which re-validates and auto-corrects all field mappings before allowing field deactivation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Data Stream field mappings are not automatically kept in sync with source schema changes; the safe sequencing is to update/remove the mapping first, then deactivate the source field, avoiding a dangling reference that surfaces as an opaque error at refresh time.\n\n* B is a factual trap: orphaned mappings are not auto-cleaned; they persist and fail at refresh, producing a 400-type error referencing the missing field.  \n* C misunderstands refresh mode: Full vs. Incremental doesn't change whether the mapping engine validates referenced source fields.  \n* D describes no such auto-correction behavior; switching refresh mode doesn't validate or fix field mappings."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "package Data Kit becomes permanently stuck in Failure status, and the documented workaround (delete and recreate) is blocked because the stream, its DLO, and DMO all have dependencies tied to the Data Kit's own metadata. What is the correct escalation path?", options: [("A", "Manually delete the underlying DMO via Data Loader to force removal, bypassing the UI dependency check."), ("B", "Engage Support to remove the Data Kit-imposed dependencies blocking deletion, then delete and recreate the DMO/DLO/stream per the documented Known Issue workaround."), ("C", "Disable the entire Data Kit managed package first, which automatically releases all component dependencies."), ("D", "Rename the stuck stream and create a parallel one with a different name, leaving the failed stream orphaned in place.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Data Kit-owned components carry backend locks that only Support/Engineering coordination with the product team can safely release before the customer can delete and recreate the stream — this matches the documented resolution path for this exact failure mode.\n\n* A is unsafe and unsupported — Data Loader/bulk deletion bypassing dependency checks risks orphaned metadata and further corruption.  \n* C is a disproportionate step (uninstalling a whole managed package) that also isn't a documented mechanism for releasing individual component dependencies.  \n* D leaves the failed stream and its underlying error state unresolved rather than fixing it, and risks confusing naming/duplicate objects downstream."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "live, an architect needs to delete five Data Streams that are no longer needed, but deletion is blocked because each stream references a shared Data Stream Template. What is the most appropriate action to unblock the go-live timeline?", options: [("A", "Delete the Data Stream Template first, which will cascade-delete all dependent streams automatically."), ("B", "Edit each stream to remove its association with the Data Stream Template, then delete each stream individually."), ("C", "Escalate to Support/Engineering since deletion is blocked by a Data Stream Template dependency that isn't resolvable from the UI, as seen in a similar documented case."), ("D", "Deactivate the streams instead of deleting them, since deactivated streams don't block a production go-live.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "When deletion is blocked by a structural dependency on a shared template that standard UI actions can't resolve, escalating to Support/Engineering for backend investigation is the correct path, consistent with how this exact scenario required an engineering investigation to unblock.\n\n* A is backwards and risky — deleting the template first is not how the dependency resolves, and cascade behavior isn't guaranteed or safe.  \n* B assumes the association can simply be edited off in the UI, but the documented case shows this specific dependency isn't resolvable that way.  \n* D avoids the deletion problem rather than solving it, and leaves stale streams in the org ahead of a go-live, which isn't acceptable when a clean cutover is required."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "A customer's sandbox refresh fails during provisioning with a unique-constraint violation on a backend ingestion job table's primary key. The architect suspects the issue is a metadata design gap rather than a data problem. What is the most likely underlying cause?", options: [("A", "The customer's Ingestion API payloads contain special characters that corrupt the primary key during export."), ("B", "The sandbox template is outdated and needs a manual metadata refresh before provisioning."), ("C", "The affected table's primary key doesn't include tenantId, so multiple tenants' rows collide under the same key during the sandbox metadata export/import."), ("D", "The customer has exceeded their sandbox storage allocation, causing partial metadata writes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A unique-key violation on a job-tracking table during multi-tenant metadata export/import points to a primary key that omits a tenant identifier, causing collisions across tenants — this matches the documented root cause for this exact error.\n\n* A conflates a different documented issue (Ingestion API special-character handling) with this metadata-export problem, which is unrelated to payload content.  \n* B is a plausible-sounding but generic fix that doesn't address the specific unique-constraint violation on file_ingestion_job_pkey.  \n* D invents a storage-limit cause with no supporting evidence; the error is a primary-key uniqueness violation, not a capacity failure."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A customer ingesting data via the Ingestion API sees intermittent 403 Forbidden errors traced to special characters in field names and file names. The architect needs a durable, scalable fix rather than a one-off cleanup. Which combination of actions best addresses this long-term?", options: [("A", "Enforce naming conventions restricting object/field/file names to letters, digits, and underscores, and introduce a cleansing/normalization step (e.g., via a cloud storage connector or middleware) ahead of ingestion."), ("B", "Increase the Ingestion API rate limit for the connected app to reduce the frequency of 403 errors."), ("C", "Ingestion API 403s are a licensing constraint tied to the number of ingestion connectors provisioned; consolidate to a single connector."), ("D", "Switch from the Ingestion API to a CRM Data Stream, since CRM streams don't enforce schema validation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The durable fix combines governance (naming conventions) with an automated pre-ingestion cleansing/normalization layer, matching the documented set of resolution options (sanitization, naming enforcement, storage-connector buffering, or middleware transformation) rather than relying on manual, one-off intervention.\n\n* B misdiagnoses the issue: 403 Forbidden is a validation/schema rejection tied to disallowed characters, not a rate-limiting/throttling response, so raising a rate limit wouldn't fix it.  \n* C invents a licensing/connector-count constraint; the documented root cause is special characters in payloads/file names, not connector provisioning limits.  \n* D is a different data source (CRM Data Stream reads Salesforce objects) and doesn't apply to file-based Ingestion API sources, and CRM streams still enforce their own schema/field validation."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "A customer's ad hoc document ingestion pipeline (individual PDF/image/CSV uploads) shows inconsistent success rates across file types and sizes. Before troubleshooting individual failures further, what should the architect evaluate first?", options: [("A", "Whether increasing the customer's Data Cloud license tier will proportionally increase ingestion success rates."), ("B", "Whether a content-repository-based ingestion approach would scale better than ad hoc one-off file uploads across the varying document types and sizes."), ("C", "Whether the Ingestion API enforces a hard cap on file size that differs by license edition."), ("D", "Whether switching the connector from batch to streaming mode eliminates document-type-dependent failures.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When inconsistency spans multiple file types and sizes in an ad hoc upload pattern, the architectural question is whether the ingestion pattern itself is fit for purpose — evaluating a content-repository-based approach addresses the scalability root cause rather than chasing individual file failures.\n\n* A assumes a licensing cause with no evidence; license tier doesn't govern per-file ingestion consistency across document types.  \n* C is a false constraint — no such edition-based hard file-size cap was identified as the driver of this inconsistency.  \n* D assumes a streaming-mode fix with no basis; the actual guidance focused on ingestion architecture (content repository vs. ad hoc uploads), not batch-vs-streaming mode."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "The Data Cloud Ingestion API returns intermittent 503 Service Unavailable and Timeout errors across both QA and production simultaneously, with no correlated code or configuration change on the customer side. What is the most appropriate architect response?", options: [("A", "Disable all scheduled ingestion jobs until the customer's development team can rewrite the integration layer."), ("B", "Immediately rebuild the customer's ingestion pipeline configuration, assuming a client-side integration defect."), ("C", "Confirm via monitoring that the 503/Timeout errors are transient platform-side degradation with no data loss, since subsequent scheduled runs completed successfully."), ("D", "Escalate to the customer that their data will need to be manually re-submitted due to permanent loss during the outage window.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Simultaneous 503/timeout errors across environments with no customer-side change is a signature of platform-side degradation; the correct response is to confirm no data loss and escalate/track with the platform engineering team, not to assume and fix a customer-side defect that doesn't exist.\n\n* A is a disproportionate reaction that halts legitimate ingestion for a transient, self-resolving platform issue, causing unnecessary business disruption.  \n* B misattributes the root cause to the customer's integration when the issue was confirmed as downstream platform-side degradation.  \n* D is factually wrong — the failures were transient with no data loss, since subsequent scheduled runs completed successfully."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "1 incident affects multiple Data 360 customers on certain instances: no data stream jobs can run due to a missing permissions policy at the platform level. As the customer's architect, what should you communicate to the customer while Support/Engineering resolves it?", options: [("A", "The customer should delete and recreate all affected data streams to force a fresh permission check."), ("B", "The customer should disable and re-enable Data 360 entirely to reset all platform integration permissions."), ("C", "Support should query for the permission set ID and reassign the \"Data 360 Salesforce Connector\" (or legacy-named equivalent) permission set to the Platform Integration User via an anonymous Apex PermissionSetAssignment insert."), ("D", "The customer should grant System Administrator profile access directly to the Platform Integration User.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "When the root cause is a platform-wide missing permissions policy applied at the instance level, the correct customer communication is that it's an infrastructure issue being fixed centrally, not something the customer's own configuration caused or can fix.\n\n* A proposes a disruptive workaround that doesn't address a policy missing at the instance level and risks unnecessary rework.  \n* B is a drastic, unsupported action — deleting/recreating streams doesn't fix a missing platform-level permissions policy tied to the instance itself.  \n* C describes escalating for a customer-visible severity level, not applying the actual fix engineering identified."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "An Enterprise Knowledge Connector extraction has failed repeatedly, and the case was reopened after an earlier attempted fix didn't hold. Before escalating further, what information should the architect ensure is gathered to enable root-cause diagnosis?", options: [("A", "Org/connection details, run status history, record counts per run, and the connector's configuration settings."), ("B", "The customer's total Data Cloud license consumption and billing tier for the current contract period."), ("C", "Whether the customer has considered switching from the Enterprise Knowledge Connector to a manual file-upload-based ingestion pipeline."), ("D", "The specific Salesforce release version number that introduced the Enterprise Knowledge Connector feature.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Diagnosing a repeatedly failing connector extraction requires connection-specific context — org/connection details, run history, record counts, and configuration — to identify patterns (e.g., partial failures, specific record types) rather than guessing at a single cause.\n\n* B misattributes a technical extraction failure to billing/licensing, which is unrelated to connector run failures.  \n* C is disproportionate and unfocused — recommending a pipeline architecture change without first diagnosing the repeated extraction failure skips necessary root-cause analysis.  \n* D is irrelevant background trivia that doesn't help diagnose a specific customer's repeated extraction failures."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "field values align and example records validate the rule — yet the records still are not unifying in production. The architect has ruled out an obvious configuration mistake. What is the appropriate next step?", options: [("A", "Conclude the match rule is fundamentally broken and rebuild the entire ruleset from scratch."), ("B", "Escalate for backend investigation, since a validated match-rule configuration with matching field values that still fails to unify points to a platform-side issue beyond visible configuration."), ("C", "Assume the customer's data volume is too large for Identity Resolution to process and recommend reducing record counts."), ("D", "Disable Identity Resolution and rely on manual deduplication going forward.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When configuration and example records validate correctly but production unification still fails, the fault likely lies in backend processing rather than visible configuration, warranting escalation for deeper investigation rather than further configuration changes.\n\n* A is premature and wasteful — rebuilding a ruleset that has already been validated as correctly configured doesn't address a suspected backend issue.  \n* C invents a volume-based limitation with no evidence; the scenario doesn't indicate a scale problem, just correctly matching records failing to unify.  \n* D abandons Identity Resolution entirely instead of pursuing root-cause diagnosis, discarding a core platform capability unnecessarily."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "An identical Identity Resolution configuration works correctly in one org but fails to unify records in another org with similar data volume. The architect has ruled out record volume as a cause. Which two areas should be investigated next as the most likely sources of the discrepancy? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The number of Chatter groups configured in each org, since IR indexes against Chatter-linked records"), ("B", "DMO/DLO mapping differences between the two orgs (e.g., a field mapped differently or missing)"), ("C", "The Salesforce org's Storage Used percentage, since IR pauses automatically above 80% storage utilization"), ("D", "Identifier data quality differences (e.g., completeness or format of matching fields) between the two orgs"), ("E", "Whether the second org's Salesforce Edition supports Identity Resolution, since IR is Enterprise Edition-only"), ("F", "Org-specific platform behavior or backend state that isn't visible in the ruleset configuration itself")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "(Note: E is also a reasonable real-world consideration per the source case, but A and C are the two areas an architect can directly investigate and act on.) Since the ruleset configuration itself is identical and volume is ruled out, the most actionable differences are in the underlying data model mapping and the quality/completeness of the identifiers being matched.\n\n* C invents a nonexistent storage-based IR throttling mechanism.  \n* E fabricates an edition-gating constraint; Identity Resolution isn't restricted by Salesforce CRM Edition in this way.  \n* A invents an irrelevant dependency on Chatter groups that has no basis in how Identity Resolution processes records."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "A customer builds a custom Identity Resolution approach using the Party Identification object to link an external \"Contacts\" DMO to Salesforce Contacts via a foreign-key field, because no direct join exists between the two DMOs. After go-live, zero records consolidate. What is the most likely root cause category?", options: [("A", "Zero consolidation always indicates the DLO-to-DMO mapping refresh has not yet run, and rerunning the mapping will resolve it automatically."), ("B", "Identity Resolution consolidation rate is capped based on the customer's license edition, and zero consolidation means the cap was reached."), ("C", "The Party Identification relationship linking the foreign-key-based DMO to Contacts is likely misconfigured, so the IR design should be reviewed against best practices for non-direct joins."), ("D", "Custom Identity Resolution using Party Identification is unsupported for non-Salesforce DMOs, so a native match rule should be used instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Using Party Identification to bridge a foreign-key relationship between DMOs is a supported, documented design pattern; a zero-consolidation result points to a misconfiguration in that relationship setup, which is resolved through careful review against best practices rather than abandoning the approach.\n\n* A oversimplifies to a single mechanical cause; zero consolidation from a relationship misconfiguration won't be fixed merely by rerunning a mapping refresh.  \n* B invents a licensing-based consolidation cap; no such edition-based hard limit governs IR consolidation rates.  \n* D is a factual trap — Party Identification-based custom IR is a supported, documented pattern for linking DMOs via non-direct (foreign-key) joins, not an unsupported configuration."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A learner following a standard Data 360 setup exercise sees an Identity Resolution error stating required fields aren't mapped for the Contact_Home stream, and a Calculated Insight built on top fails because its source object is in an error state. What is the correct sequence to resolve this dependency chain?", options: [("A", "Republish the Calculated Insight first to force the platform to auto-map any missing IR fields retroactively."), ("B", "Rebuild the Calculated Insight using a different source DMO that doesn't depend on unified profiles."), ("C", "Increase the Identity Resolution batch size setting, since the error indicates an IR performance timeout rather than a mapping gap."), ("D", "Map the Phone field to the Formatted E164 Phone Number (Contact Point Phone) attribute in the stream mapping, refresh the stream, rerun the ruleset, then republish the Calculated Insight.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The dependency chain runs stream mapping → Identity Resolution → Calculated Insight, so fixes must be applied in that order: correct the field mapping, refresh the stream, rerun IR, then republish the CI so it reads a healthy source.\n\n* A reverses the dependency direction; publishing a CI cannot retroactively fix a missing field mapping upstream in IR — the CI depends on IR completing successfully, not the other way around.  \n* B avoids the actual fix and discards the unified-profile approach the learner is meant to build, rather than resolving the mapping gap.  \n* C misdiagnoses the error as a performance/timeout issue; the actual error (\"Map all required fields\") is a mapping-completeness gap, not a batch-size/timeout problem."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "Using the Data Graph Query API, an architect observes that segment membership information for the Individual DMO (Individual - Latest) only appears in the Data Graph after an Identity Resolution run completes, with higher credit consumption noted during those runs. What should the architect factor into design guidance for the customer?", options: [("A", "Segment membership materialization for the Individual DMO in the Data Graph is dependent on Identity Resolution runs, so IR scheduling frequency directly affects when membership data becomes queryable."), ("B", "Segment membership always appears in the Data Graph in real time regardless of Identity Resolution status."), ("C", "Data Graph Query API results never include segment membership fields for the Individual DMO under any configuration."), ("D", "Segment membership only materializes if the Data Graph is rebuilt manually after every segment publish, independent of Identity Resolution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Because segment membership only materializes after IR runs (per the documented behavior), the architect must design IR scheduling with both data-freshness needs and credit-consumption cost in mind — the two are linked, not independent.\n\n* B overstates the limitation — the documented issue is a dependency on IR, not a total absence of segment membership support in Data Graphs.  \n* C ignores the documented behavior that membership does appear, just conditionally on IR having run.  \n* D invents a manual-rebuild requirement; the observed dependency is on IR execution, not on manually forcing a Data Graph rebuild after every publish."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "tenured customer has chronic Data 360 issues: production deployments performed via unsupported file-based metadata changes (rather than managed Data Kits) have corrupted org integrity, foundational mapping errors persist, and Identity Resolution consolidation sits at only 3–7%. As the architect, what is the most appropriate strategic recommendation?", options: [("A", "Provide another round of advisory documentation and best-practice links, since the customer's team simply needs more self-service guidance."), ("B", "Escalate for a Strategic Customer Investment request to bring in hands-on Professional Services to rebuild the CI/CD pipeline using managed Data Kits and fix the data foundation directly."), ("C", "Recommend the customer purchase additional Data Cloud license capacity to resolve the low IR consolidation rate."), ("D", "Instruct the customer to switch entirely from managed Data Kits to file-based metadata deployment for more granular control.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When chronic, compounding issues stem from unsupported deployment practices and deep data-foundation problems, advisory guidance alone has already proven insufficient — hands-on Professional Services to rebuild the pipeline and directly execute the fixes is the appropriate escalation, matching the documented resolution path for this exact Red Account scenario.\n\n* A repeats an approach already shown to be inadequate for a customer at this level of chronic risk; advisory-only guidance hasn't resolved the underlying deployment and data-hygiene issues.  \n* C misdiagnoses the root cause as an ingestion/license-capacity constraint rather than deployment methodology, data hygiene, and mapping errors.  \n* D is backwards — unsupported file-based metadata deployment is what corrupted org integrity in the first place; the fix is to move to managed Data Kits, not away from them."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "An architect is designing Identity Resolution for a B2B customer where Contacts must unify at the Individual level, but the customer also needs Account-level entity resolution across multiple source systems where account names vary (e.g., \"Acme Inc.\" vs \"ACME Incorporated\"). Which statement correctly describes how to achieve both in Data 360?", options: [("A", "Build a single Calculated Insight that flags likely duplicate account names, since Data 360 does not support entity resolution for non-Individual objects."), ("B", "Rely on standard Individual-based Identity Resolution match rules, since all IR ruleset types apply uniformly across DMOs regardless of entity type."), ("C", "Configure a custom Identity Resolution ruleset using Party Identification to link and reconcile the B2B account-level records, following the same non-direct-join design pattern used for individual-level custom IR."), ("D", "Use a Data Transform to deduplicate account records nightly, since IR cannot be extended to any object outside the standard Individual/Contact model.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Each Identity Resolution ruleset in Data 360 resolves to one unified DMO; unifying both Individuals and an Account-like entity requires two distinct rulesets, each scoped and tuned (including fuzzy match rules) for its own object.\n\n* A is factually wrong; Data 360 does support match/reconciliation rules against non-Individual DMOs such as Party Identification-based entities, so a single ad hoc CI isn't required or optimal.  \n* B incorrectly assumes IR ruleset types are entity-agnostic; account/B2B-level unification needs its own ruleset design, not a copy of Individual-level rules.  \n* D overstates a limitation — IR can be extended to non-standard entities via Party Identification-based custom configurations, so a nightly Data Transform workaround isn't the platform-intended approach."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "A marketing team needs to activate a segment of 2 million high-value customers to Marketing Cloud Engagement nightly, but also needs a real-time trigger to fire a Data Action the moment a customer's cart value crosses a threshold during a browsing session. Which combination of Data 360 capabilities should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use Data Cloud Flow Triggers exclusively, since Segments cannot activate to Marketing Cloud Engagement."), ("B", "Configure the segment's activation frequency to \"Real-Time\" so records sync immediately, bypassing standard batch publish behavior."), ("C", "Enable DISABLE_SHARED_OBJECTS on the org so the Data Extension is created in a dedicated, non-shared folder."), ("D", "Check whether DISABLE_SHARED_OBJECTS is enabled for the org, since that setting affects whether shared DE folders are used correctly and can cause activations to create a target DE with 0 populated records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Batch segmentation with a scheduled activation is the right tool for a recurring, large-scale audience sync to MCE, while a real-time/streaming trigger (via a streaming Data Transform or condition on a Calculated Insight) is needed to react instantly to an in-session event like a cart-value threshold.\n\n* A incorrectly claims Segments can't activate to MCE — that is a standard, supported activation target, and Flow Triggers serve a different purpose.  \n* B is a right-feature/wrong-mechanism trap: activation frequency controls sync timing, not whether records populate the DE folder correctly.  \n* C reverses the fix direction implied by the documented case — the setting's state (not simply enabling it) is what needs to be checked against the shared-folder behavior causing the issue."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "A financial services customer requires that PII fields (SSN, account number) ingested into Data 360 be masked for most internal users but visible in full to a small compliance team. The architect also needs Row-Level Security so that regional analysts only see records from their own region. Which combination correctly implements both requirements?", options: [("A", "Apply Field-Level Security via masking/tokenization policies on the PII fields, and separately configure Row-Level Security (RLS) rules scoped by region on the DMO to restrict which records each business unit's users can query."), ("B", "Apply Shield Platform Encryption to the underlying DLOs, since it natively extends to Data 360's data model objects the same way it does to CRM objects."), ("C", "Rely on Data Space separation alone, since each Data Space automatically enforces both field masking and regional row-level restrictions without additional configuration."), ("D", "Configure a single Data Action that redacts PII fields for all users uniformly, since Data 360 does not support differentiated field-level access by user or role.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Data 360 separates these concerns: masking/tokenization policies handle field-level PII protection, while Row-Level Security rules (typically driven by a security predicate on a DMO) independently control which records a user can see — together they satisfy both requirements without conflating them.\n\n* B is a deprecated/legacy-approach trap: Shield Platform Encryption applies to Salesforce CRM objects, not Data 360's DLOs/DMOs, so it wouldn't cover this data model layer.  \n* C overstates what Data Spaces provide — they isolate data by space but don't automatically apply field-level masking or row-level regional restrictions; those still need explicit configuration.  \n* D understates platform capability — Data 360 does support field- and row-level access differentiation, so a single uniform redaction Data Action isn't necessary or correct."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A telecom wants to trigger a Next Best Action flow in Agentforce the moment a Calculated Insight flags a customer as \"high churn risk,\" using near-real-time data rather than waiting for the next batch cycle. Which two components should the architect combine to meet this requirement? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A Segment scheduled to refresh every 15 minutes as the sole trigger mechanism"), ("B", "An Einstein Prediction Builder model deployed directly against the DLO layer"), ("C", "A Data Action configured with a Calculated Insight-based trigger condition"), ("D", "A batch Data Transform scheduled hourly to approximate real-time behavior"), ("E", "A streaming Data Transform feeding the source DMOs used by the Calculated Insight so it reflects near-real-time signals"), ("F", "A Flow or Agentforce action subscribed to the Data Action's outbound event")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "A Data Action with a trigger condition tied to the Calculated Insight is the mechanism that detects the flagged state, and a Flow/Agentforce action subscribed to that Data Action's outbound event is what actually fires the Next Best Action — together they form the real-time trigger-to-action chain.\n\n* D fails because hourly batch scheduling is not real-time and defeats the stated \"near-real-time\" requirement — it's a right-outcome/wrong-mechanism trap.  \n* E alone doesn't trigger anything; it only improves data freshness upstream of the CI, so it doesn't satisfy the triggering requirement by itself.  \n* B misapplies Einstein Prediction Builder, which operates on DMOs/CRM data for scoring, not as a real-time trigger mechanism against raw DLOs.  \n* A is a scope-limit trap: segment refresh scheduling is still batch-based, and segments aren't designed as the sole real-time trigger for individual customer events."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "A nonprofit customer using NPSP wants to bring Fundraising objects into Data 360 via a CRM Data Stream. All relevant profiles have object-level Read and View All access, yet the data stream setup screen still reports the Fundraising objects as unavailable. What is the most likely root cause and correct fix?", options: [("A", "The Data Cloud license doesn't support NPSP data at all; the customer must use the Ingestion API instead."), ("B", "The hidden Data Cloud integration user needs the specific system permission for the license-gated feature (e.g., Fundraising User) enabled directly on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set, since industry Permission Set Licenses assigned to it aren't sufficient."), ("C", "The customer needs to grant System Administrator profile access to the Data Cloud integration user to see all license-gated objects."), ("D", "NPSP Fundraising objects require a separate, dedicated CRM Data Stream connector distinct from the standard Data Cloud Salesforce Connector.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "License-gated industry objects require the specific system permission enabled directly on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set used by the hidden integration user — object-level Read/View-All alone isn't sufficient because the gating permission is separate from standard object security.\n\n* A is a factual trap; Data 360 does support NPSP/Fundraising objects via CRM Data Streams once permissions are correctly configured.  \n* C violates a least-privilege/governance constraint — granting full System Administrator access to the integration user is an over-broad, unsupported workaround for a specific permission gap.  \n* D invents a connector requirement; NPSP objects still flow through the standard Data Cloud Salesforce Connector, not a separate dedicated connector."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "After months of stable operation, a production DMO stops being recognized by the Data 360 query engine — queries referencing it fail with \"DMO not found,\" even though the DMO's definition looks correct in Data Model setup. No recent deployments or field changes were made. What is the recommended first troubleshooting step before escalating?", options: [("A", "Escalate directly to Engineering, since query engine metadata issues cannot be resolved by an admin."), ("B", "Delete and recreate the DMO from scratch, since a corrupted DMO cannot be repaired in place."), ("C", "Edit and re-save the DMO, even with no real change, to trigger a metadata cache refresh, since this resolved an identical documented \"DMO not found\" symptom."), ("D", "Rebuild the underlying DLO and remap it to the DMO, since query engine errors always originate from the DLO layer, not the DMO itself.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A stale metadata cache is a known, low-risk cause of \"DMO not found\" errors; re-saving the DMO forces a metadata cache refresh without any destructive change, and is the appropriate first step before deeper investigation.\n\n* A skips a simple, safe self-service step that resolves this exact symptom in documented cases, before escalation is warranted.  \n* B is an overreaction — the DMO was properly defined, so deletion/recreation risks unnecessary downstream disruption (dependent mappings, CIs, segments) for a problem that's actually a cache issue.  \n* D assumes the fault lies in the DLO layer with no evidence; the documented root cause was a query-engine metadata cache issue on the DMO itself."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "A retailer's segment of 9 million loyalty members experiences activation publishing delays. Investigation shows delays correlate with concurrent activation jobs writing to a shared, curated Loyalty DMO that has data skew from repeated identifier values. Which two actions should the architect recommend to address the root cause? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Increase the segment's refresh frequency to hourly so smaller batches reduce contention"), ("B", "Convert the DMO from a curated DMO to a Data Graph, since Data Graphs are not subject to write locks"), ("C", "Reduce the segment size to under 1 million members so activation always completes within the batch window"), ("D", "Stagger or serialize concurrent activation jobs that write against the shared Loyalty DMO to avoid write-lock contention"), ("E", "Work with the data engineering team to address the data skew (repeated identifier values) in the curated Loyalty DMO"), ("F", "Switch the segment's activation target from Marketing Cloud Engagement to the Ingestion API to bypass locking")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [4, 5], explanation: "The documented root cause was write-lock contention from concurrent jobs plus data skew on the shared DMO; staggering/serializing the concurrent writes and fixing the underlying identifier skew both directly address that mechanism.\n\n* C is a padded, ineffective distractor — segment size was explicitly ruled out as the cause here.  \n* F misunderstands activation targets; the Ingestion API is for inbound ingestion, not an alternative activation destination.  \n* A would likely worsen contention by increasing the frequency of concurrent writes against the same shared DMO.  \n* B is an invalid-mechanism trap: Data Graphs are a query/serving layer over DMOs, not a replacement for a curated DMO."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "backed Data Streams, each mapped to a different DMO, all connected through a single shared Azure connector. Streams intermittently fail with vague \"Backfill failed job stats\" errors whenever two or more refresh schedules overlap. What is the best long-term architectural fix, beyond simply staggering the schedules?", options: [("A", "Switch all four streams to the Ingestion API, since file-based ingestion doesn't have connector task limits."), ("B", "Increase the Azure connector's polling frequency so retries happen faster after a 422 rejection."), ("C", "Split each DMO into two smaller DMOs to reduce the payload size per sync task."), ("D", "Stagger the four streams' refresh schedules to avoid collisions, since the shared Azure connector only allows one non-terminal sync task at a time and concurrent refreshes trigger HTTP 422 rejections.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The root cause is that a single connector permits only one non-terminal sync task at a time; giving each DMO its own connector (or serializing requests) removes the structural bottleneck rather than just avoiding it via scheduling.\n\n* A swaps one ingestion mechanism for another without evidence it's appropriate for this Azure-sourced case, and doesn't resolve the described root cause — the shared connector's single-task limitation.  \n* B misdiagnoses the mechanism — faster polling doesn't prevent the underlying single-non-terminal-task constraint from rejecting concurrent syncs; it just retries the same collision sooner.  \n* C addresses payload size, not concurrency — the failures stem from simultaneous sync tasks colliding on one connector, not payload size limits."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "A global retailer operating under GDPR and CCPA needs its Data 360 implementation to respect consent preferences captured in multiple source systems before any segment can activate a customer to an outbound marketing channel. Which two capabilities should the architect leverage? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A single global consent flag on the Individual DMO that ignores channel-specific or source-system differences"), ("B", "Contact Point-level consent management with unified consent status flowing into segmentation criteria"), ("C", "Data Space isolation as the sole mechanism for enforcing consent, since separating by region satisfies GDPR/CCPA"), ("D", "Segment exclusion/suppression logic that filters out individuals lacking valid opt-in consent for the given channel before activation"), ("E", "Relying on the destination platform (e.g., MCE) to independently filter unconsented contacts post-activation"), ("F", "Einstein Trust Layer configuration, since it governs consent for generative AI prompts rather than marketing consent")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "Consent should be modeled at the Contact Point/channel level and unified across sources, then actively used as suppression/exclusion criteria in segmentation before activation — this ensures unconsented individuals are never sent downstream.\n\n* C misapplies Data Spaces, a tenant/regional data-partitioning mechanism, as a substitute for consent enforcement.  \n* E is a governance risk trap: deferring consent enforcement to the destination platform means unconsented data has already left Data 360.  \n* F is an adjacent-product trap: the Einstein Trust Layer governs AI/LLM data usage and grounding, not marketing consent management.  \n* A oversimplifies a real requirement — channel-specific consent (e.g., opted into email but not SMS) is standard and a single flag can't represent it.\n\n*Study set generated for based on the official Data Cloud (Data 360) Consultant exam guide's weighted sections: Data Modeling & Data Streams, Identity Resolution, Segmentation & Activation, Data Governance & Security, Data Actions/AI, and Calculated Insights. August 2026.*"),
        Question(id: "29", question: "A customer's DLO-to-DMO mapping fails intermittently, with no consistent error pattern across attempts. Before escalating, which sequence of self-service checks should the architect have the customer perform?", options: [("A", "Retry the mapping (since it's often transient), check for null targetField values among the mapped fields, and try resyncing the DMO by opening and re-saving it without changes."), ("B", "Escalate immediately to Engineering, since intermittent DLO-to-DMO mapping failures always indicate a backend platform defect."), ("C", "Delete and recreate both the DLO and DMO from scratch, since intermittent mapping failures indicate underlying object corruption."), ("D", "Switch the mapping to use a Calculated Insight instead of a direct DLO-to-DMO mapping, since CIs don't have this failure mode.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The documented low-risk self-service sequence — retry, check for null targetField values, and resync via re-save — resolves the majority of intermittent mapping failures without destructive action or unnecessary escalation.\n\n* B skips reasonable self-service diagnostics that resolve this class of issue in documented cases, before assuming a platform defect requiring escalation.  \n* C assumes object corruption with no evidence; the documented workarounds address transient mapping failures without needing to delete and recreate the objects.  \n* D proposes an unrelated architectural change (CI instead of direct mapping) that doesn't address the specific intermittent mapping failure and introduces unnecessary complexity."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "A large customer's org has grown to roughly 5,700 DMOs. Suddenly, all Data Model tooling — DMO creation, Relationships, Calculated Insights, mapping, and Segments — begins throwing a generic internal server error across the entire org, with no recent deployments or package installs identified as a trigger. What should the architect suspect and investigate first?", options: [("A", "A corrupted individual DMO is cascading errors to all other Data Model features; isolate and delete the most recently created DMO."), ("B", "Possible breach of a DMO count limit/threshold triggering the server error across all Data Model operations, given the org's very high DMO count."), ("C", "The org's Data Cloud edition was recently downgraded, removing access to Data Model tooling entirely."), ("D", "A recent deployment or managed package install introduced a conflicting schema change across all DMOs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When every Data Model feature fails simultaneously at a very high DMO count with no deployment trigger, a count-limit/threshold breach is the leading suspected cause to investigate, consistent with the documented case at this scale.\n\n* A incorrectly assumes a single-DMO root cause for an org-wide, cross-feature failure pattern; the error affected every core modeling feature simultaneously, not one isolated object.  \n* C invents an edition-change explanation with no supporting evidence in the case; edition downgrades don't typically manifest as a generic internal server error across all Data Model operations.  \n* D was specifically ruled out — no recent deployments or package installs were identified as a trigger."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "A healthcare customer's Data Kit deployment triggers cascading failures across Calculated Insights, Data Graphs, Data Transforms, and Identity Resolution because some existing objects lack required mappings and certain DLOs aren't available in the target org, with one blocking DLO itself stuck in an error state and undeletable. What is the most appropriate resolution approach?", options: [("A", "Reduce the Data Kit's scope by removing all Identity Resolution components, since IR is the most likely source of the cascading failure."), ("B", "Force-delete the blocking DLO via direct database access to unblock the deployment queue."), ("C", "Engage the CDP Core Data Streams Scrum team via an engineering investigation, since existing objects lack required mappings, some DLOs aren't available in the target org, and one blocking DLO is stuck in an error state and can't be deleted through normal means."), ("D", "Roll back to a previous Data Kit version that doesn't include Calculated Insights or Data Graphs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "When a DLO is stuck in an error state and can't be deleted through normal means, and multiple downstream features are cascading-failing from missing mappings/DLOs, engineering engagement with the responsible product team is the correct path — matching the documented resolution for this exact multi-feature cascading failure.\n\n* A misdiagnoses the cause; the described failures span multiple features (CIs, Data Graphs, Transforms), not specifically IR, so removing IR wouldn't address the underlying mapping/availability gaps.  \n* B is unsafe and unsupported — direct database manipulation to force-delete a blocked object bypasses platform integrity checks and risks further corruption.  \n* D avoids the problem instead of resolving it, and discards Data Kit functionality the customer needs rather than fixing the missing mappings and unavailable DLOs."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "any new field mapping or mapping update on that pairing throws \"Unable to save, please try again,\" while all other DLO/DMO pairings work fine. Investigation reveals a DMO interface field (e.g., a boolean-like flag) was originally created with the wrong data type (Number instead of Text), a defect since fixed for new DMOs in a later platform release but not retroactively. What is the correct remediation?", options: [("A", "Create a new DMO from scratch with all fields recreated, since a single field's data type cannot be changed once set."), ("B", "Change the DLO's field data type to match, since the DMO interface field's type is authoritative and cannot be altered."), ("C", "File a defect requesting a retroactive platform fix, since release 260.11 was supposed to auto-correct existing mistyped DMO interface fields."), ("D", "Delete and recreate the mistyped DMO interface field (e.g., changing it from Number to Text) on the affected DMO, then retry the mapping.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Since the platform fix only prevents the mismatch on new DMOs and doesn't retroactively correct existing ones, the documented remediation is to delete and recreate the specific mistyped field on the affected DMO, which resolves the mapping failure without a full DMO rebuild.\n\n* A is an overcorrection; only the single mistyped field needs to be fixed, not the entire DMO, and fields can be deleted/recreated individually.  \n* B misdiagnoses which side needs correction; the documented root cause is the DMO interface field's type being wrong, not the DLO's field type.  \n* C misunderstands the release fix's scope — 260.11 prevents the mismatch on new DMOs going forward but explicitly does not retroactively correct existing ones."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "A customer complains that Data Processing Engine (DPE) only surfaces Calculated Insight validation errors when the entire flow is saved, rather than incrementally as each transformation node is added, making debugging difficult. What architecture pattern should the architect recommend to reduce this troubleshooting pain going forward?", options: [("A", "Do heavy transformation work upfront using a Batch Transform into a DLO, map that DLO to a DMO, then load into the CRM object with one object load per definition, rather than relying on DPE's own transformation troubleshooting."), ("B", "Split every transformation into a separate DPE flow so each node's errors surface independently at save time."), ("C", "Disable DPE's save-time validation entirely so errors surface only at runtime, giving more granular error context."), ("D", "Move all transformation logic into a Calculated Insight instead of DPE, since CIs report errors immediately when a transformation node is added.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Moving heavy transformation work upstream (Batch Transform → DLO → DMO → CRM object) reduces dependence on DPE's own error surfacing, since the transformation logic is validated and resolved before ever reaching DPE — this is the documented recommended pattern for this exact pain point.\n\n* B is a fabricated claim; splitting flows doesn't inherently change when DPE validates Calculated Insight errors — they still surface at save time.  \n* C removes a safety check rather than solving the debugging-difficulty problem, and pushes validation failures to runtime instead, which is riskier.  \n* D confuses DPE (Data Processing Engine transformations) with Calculated Insights, which have a different error-surfacing model and aren't a drop-in replacement for DPE transformation logic."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A Marketing Cloud Next customer's Scoring Model publish fails, and the associated Data Graph throws \"doesn't contain valid personalization information.\" Following documented guidance, the customer adds the Email Address field from Contact Point Email to a new Data Graph, but the error persists. What should the architect investigate next?", options: [("A", "Assume the Data Graph feature itself is broken for Marketing Cloud Next customers and file a product defect immediately."), ("B", "Check whether an underlying Data Stream or DMO (such as Party Identification) is in an error state, since the documented root cause was a failed upstream source rather than the Data Graph's field configuration."), ("C", "Remove the Data Graph entirely and rebuild the Scoring Model without any Data Graph dependency."), ("D", "Add every available Contact Point Email field to the Data Graph simultaneously, since the error is resolved by maximizing field coverage.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When the standard documented fix (adding the personalization field) doesn't resolve the error, the next step is to check whether an upstream dependency — a source Data Stream or DMO the Data Graph relies on — is itself in an error state, since a Data Graph can't surface valid personalization data from a broken source.\n\n* A jumps to a product-defect conclusion without first ruling out a known root-cause category (upstream source errors) documented for this exact symptom.  \n* C is a disproportionate response that removes needed functionality rather than diagnosing the actual failed upstream object.  \n* D misdiagnoses the fix mechanism — adding the Email field from Contact Point Email was already tried and the error persisted, since the deeper cause was an upstream Data Stream/DMO error state."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "A System Administrator with full permissions and the ability to query all included DMOs via the Query Editor still sees \"You can't view or create this Data Graph because you don't have access to all objects and fields\" when opening the Data Graph builder. What should the architect suspect, and what is the recommended first remediation step?", options: [("A", "Data Graphs require a dedicated Data Graph License separate from the Data Cloud license; verify the license is provisioned."), ("B", "A genuine governance issue exists; the System Admin's profile lacks Data Graph-specific permissions distinct from standard object/field access."), ("C", "This is likely a backend/metadata state issue rather than a true access problem, given the admin can query all included DMOs successfully via the Query Editor; try Update Status and Retry from the Data Graph UI before escalating."), ("D", "The error indicates the Data Graph references a deprecated DMO relationship type that must be rebuilt using the newer relationship framework.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Since a System Admin already has full access and can query all included DMOs successfully, a real permissions gap is ruled out; the documented pattern is a backend/metadata state issue, and the recommended first step is \"Update Status\"/\"Retry\" from the UI before escalating further.\n\n* A invents a licensing requirement not indicated anywhere in the scenario or documented pattern for this symptom.  \n* B incorrectly asserts a stricter separate permission model than what's described; the admin already has full query access to all included DMOs, undermining a true governance-gap explanation.  \n* D invents a deprecated relationship-type explanation with no basis; the documented pattern points to a backend/metadata state issue, not a schema deprecation."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "production/test environment gets stuck in a \"Creating\" state indefinitely and never completes, a recurring pattern the architect has seen before in similar environments. What is the correct escalation sequence?", options: [("A", "Delete the Realtime Data Graph and recreate it as a standard (non-Realtime) Data Graph, since Realtime Data Graphs are not supported in non-prod environments."), ("B", "Escalate immediately to the Data Graph secondary on-call without attempting any self-service recovery first."), ("C", "Increase the org's Data Graph concurrency limit, since \"Creating\" state is caused by too many simultaneous Data Graph builds."), ("D", "Attempt Update Status and Retry from the Data Graph UI first, as standard recovery for this recurring pattern; escalate to the Data Graph secondary on-call only if that doesn't resolve it.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The documented standard recovery sequence for a stuck \"Creating\" state is to try \"Update Status\" and \"Retry\" from the UI first, escalating to the Data Graph secondary on-call only if that doesn't resolve it — balancing self-service speed with appropriate escalation.\n\n* A invents an unsupported environment restriction; Realtime Data Graphs are not documented as prohibited in non-prod environments.  \n* B assumes automatic escalation without first trying the documented self-service recovery step, wasting on-call time for an issue that often resolves via UI actions.  \n* C invents a concurrency-limit explanation with no documented basis for the \"Creating\" state symptom."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A production Data Graph cannot be deleted, and the architect sees no obviously visible dependencies in the UI. Based on documented patterns for this exact symptom, what should be checked before escalating, and what is the less common alternate cause?", options: [("A", "Check for remaining dependencies (linked content, activations, or segments still referencing the graph) first; the less common alternate cause is an internal API error where UI-based deletion succeeds but API-based deletion fails."), ("B", "Assume a row-count limit was exceeded on the underlying DMOs and reduce data volume before retrying deletion."), ("C", "Assume the Data Graph is protected because it's tied to an active contract term and cannot be deleted until renewal."), ("D", "Rebuild the entire Data Graph from scratch with a different name, then delete the original once the replacement is confirmed working.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The documented pattern for this exact symptom is that deletion blocks usually come from remaining dependencies (activations, segments, linked content) not visible at first glance, with a less common alternate cause being a UI/API deletion-path discrepancy — both should be checked before escalating.\n\n* B fabricates a row-count-based deletion restriction with no basis in the documented pattern for blocked Data Graph deletion.  \n* C invents a contract-expiration mechanism unrelated to the documented dependency-based blocking behavior.  \n* D is an unnecessary workaround that doesn't address the root dependency block and risks leaving two overlapping Data Graphs temporarily active."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "Multiple CRM Data Stream refreshes across an org begin failing, all citing missing fields or objects, even though the relevant profiles and the customer's own admin access look correct. The architect suspects the hidden Platform Integration User lost a specific permission set. What is the correct diagnostic and remediation sequence?", options: [("A", "Grant \"Modify All Data\" to every profile in the org, since broad object permissions bypass any missing-permission-set issue."), ("B", "Query for the permission set ID (SELECT Id FROM PermissionSet WHERE label = 'Data 360 Salesforce Connector') and reassign it to the Platform Integration User via an anonymous Apex PermissionSetAssignment insert."), ("C", "Recreate the Platform Integration User entirely, since a corrupted user record is the most common cause of missing field/object permissions."), ("D", "Have the customer manually re-map every field on the affected CRM Data Streams, since missing fields indicate a mapping configuration error.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The documented recurring root cause for broad CRM stream failures citing missing fields/objects is the hidden Platform Integration User losing its connector permission set; querying for the permission set and reassigning it via an anonymous Apex insert is the correct, targeted fix.\n\n* A is an overbroad, insecure workaround that doesn't specifically address the missing connector permission set and violates least-privilege practices by granting unrelated org-wide data access.  \n* C invents an unsupported user-corruption cause; the documented root cause is the permission set being removed, not the hidden integration user itself being corrupted.  \n* D misdiagnoses the failure layer; the documented cause is a missing permission set on the connector user, not incorrect field mappings on the streams."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "level Read/View-All access granted on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set for Fundraising objects, and the objects are fully queryable in Salesforce, yet Data 360 still reports \"Object unavailable\" when creating a data stream. What is the root cause and fix?", options: [("A", "The customer must purchase an additional Data Cloud add-on SKU specifically for NPSP object support."), ("B", "Object-level Read/View-All access needs to be granted directly to the integration user's individual login rather than through a permission set."), ("C", "The hidden Data Cloud integration user needs the specific system permission for the license-gated feature enabled directly on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set, since industry Permission Set Licenses assigned to it aren't sufficient."), ("D", "The Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set must be cloned and renamed before it can grant access to license-gated industry objects.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "License-gated industry features require their specific system permission enabled directly on the connector permission set used by the hidden integration user; because that user cannot hold Permission Set Licenses, object-level access alone (even with View-All) is insufficient without this extra step.\n\n* A invents a licensing/SKU requirement not indicated in the documented root cause, which was a missing system permission on the connector permission set, not a missing add-on purchase.  \n* B misdirects to individual-login-level access grants, which isn't how the documented fix worked and bypasses standard permission-set-based administration.  \n* D invents an unnecessary cloning step; the fix is enabling the specific system permission directly on the existing Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set, not duplicating it."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "services vertical can't see the FinancialDeal object when creating a CRM data stream, despite having reviewed and confirmed standard object permissions. Based on a documented similar pattern (e.g., Fundraising), what should the architect check?", options: [("A", "Whether the customer's org language/locale setting is blocking the object from appearing in stream creation."), ("B", "Whether the object needs to first be added via a custom managed package before it becomes visible to Data Cloud."), ("C", "Whether the object's Salesforce API version is incompatible with the current Data Cloud connector version."), ("D", "Whether the specific industry system permission for that object (e.g., \"FSC Deal Management Platform User\") is enabled on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set, similar to the Fundraising case pattern.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "This mirrors the documented Fundraising pattern: license-gated industry objects require their specific system permission enabled directly on the connector permission set for the hidden integration user, not just standard object-level security.\n\n* A misattributes the cause to locale/language settings, which have no documented connection to object visibility in stream creation.  \n* B fabricates a managed-package prerequisite; FinancialDeal is a standard FSC object exposed via permission-set-gated system permissions, not custom package installation.  \n* C invents an API-version-compatibility explanation with no basis in the documented pattern, which points to a specific system permission gap instead."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "An FSC Data Kit deployment repeatedly fails on several relationship objects with SFDC_PERMISSION_ERROR, even after the architect confirms via direct SOQL that the connector permission set has full read/view-all access on all flagged objects, restarts Core, waits out possible cache TTLs, and runs a tenant metadata sync Black Tab action. What should the architect conclude and do next?", options: [("A", "Conclude the standard permission-set fix has been ruled out via direct verification, and escalate to the core/off-core data-services team for the specific off-core permission-check/cache-propagation sync mechanism needed."), ("B", "Grant \"View All Data\" org-wide to bypass the object-level permission checks entirely."), ("C", "Reinstall the FSC Data Kit from scratch, since a corrupted Data Kit package is the most likely cause of a persistent SFDC_PERMISSION_ERROR."), ("D", "Assume the SOQL verification was inaccurate and re-grant the same permission set again through the UI.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "When the standard permission-set fix has been directly verified as correct via SOQL, and Core restarts, cache TTL waits, and tenant metadata sync haven't resolved it, the documented next step is escalating to the core/off-core data-services team for a deeper sync mechanism — the issue lies beyond standard permission-set configuration.\n\n* B abandons required functionality without addressing the actual root cause, which is a deeper platform sync gap, not a Data Kit defect.  \n* C recommends an unsafe, unsupported bypass around a permission-check/cache gap rather than the proper sync mechanism.  \n* D invents an unrelated cause; the scenario already confirms permission sets are correctly granted, so the fix lies elsewhere."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "A sandbox can't enable Data 360 because the org requires deleting an existing Agentforce Data Library first, but that Data Library shows no visible records in the UI — a known pattern of orphaned backend metadata not visible in the UI. What is the suspected fix path?", options: [("A", "Recreate the Agentforce Data Library with the same name so the system recognizes it as already deleted."), ("B", "Enable a Support-only org permission (e.g., SfdcInternalQAClients) via a Black Tab action to allow cleanup of orphaned backend metadata not visible in the UI."), ("C", "Grant the customer's admin View Setup and Configuration permission, since that is what's blocking visibility of the Data Library."), ("D", "Have the customer manually delete all Agentforce-related permission sets to force the Data Library reference to clear.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The documented suspected fix for this exact orphaned-metadata pattern is enabling a Support-only internal org permission via a Black Tab action, which allows backend cleanup of metadata that isn't visible or manageable through the standard UI.\n\n* A doesn't address orphaned backend metadata; recreating a UI-visible library with the same name won't resolve a backend-only artifact.  \n* C misdiagnoses the constraint as a viewing permission, when the documented cause is metadata existing in the backend without a UI-manageable path to delete it.  \n* D is a disproportionate, unsupported workaround that risks breaking unrelated Agentforce permission configuration."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "A customer setting up Tableau Next reports the \"Load Data\" step for bringing standard objects into Data 360 getting stuck at 0% indefinitely, even after stopping/restarting the process and toggling Tableau Next off and on. What is the appropriate next step for the architect?", options: [("A", "Recommend the customer purchase additional Data Cloud storage capacity, since \"Load Data\" stalls are typically a storage capacity issue."), ("B", "Conclude the stall is expected behavior and will resolve itself within 48 hours without any action."), ("C", "Escalate to the swarm channel to check for a known issue, since restarting the process and toggling Tableau Next off and on did not resolve the stall."), ("D", "Have the customer manually recreate all standard objects as custom objects to bypass the \"Load Data\" step entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Since the documented self-service steps (restart, toggle off/on) were already tried without success, escalating to the swarm channel to check for a known issue is the appropriate next step, consistent with how this exact case was handled.\n\n* A invents a storage-capacity root cause with no evidence connecting it to this symptom.  \n* B fabricates an expected-duration claim with no support; the documented case shows indefinite stalling even after standard recovery attempts.  \n* D is a disproportionate workaround that abandons the intended standard-object load path rather than addressing the stuck \"Load Data\" step."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "A customer repeatedly fails to install the C360 Semantic Model app during the \"Load Data\" step, even after reinstalling from Platform Tools. The architect suspects the install flow may not be checking for the latest app version. What should be verified first?", options: [("A", "Whether the customer has enough API call allocation remaining for the day, since app installs consume API calls exclusively."), ("B", "Whether the customer's org is on Lightning Experience, since the C360 Semantic Model app requires it for installation."), ("C", "Whether the customer has an active support contract tier that includes app installation assistance."), ("D", "Whether the \"Load Data\" install flow is actually checking for and installing the latest available app version, since reinstalling from Platform Tools didn't resolve the failure.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Given the architect's stated suspicion and the documented open question in this case, verifying whether the install flow is actually pulling the latest app version (versus a stale reference) is the direct next diagnostic step for a persistent \"Load Data\" install failure.\n\n* A invents an API-allocation cause with no connection to app installation version-checking behavior described in the case.  \n* B fabricates a Lightning Experience requirement not indicated in the documented symptom or troubleshooting.  \n* C is irrelevant to the technical failure mode; support contract tier doesn't affect whether the install flow checks the correct app version."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "authorized Data 360 instance not intended for GovCloud Civilian use, though the instance was never accessed and no customer data was impacted. What is the correct customer-facing remediation process?", options: [("A", "Notify affected customers by email, and in Data Cloud Setup under Data Cloud Lifecycle Management → Clean Up Your Org, have them review deletion details, check the consent box, and click \"I Approve Data Deletion\" to trigger reprovisioning into the correct instance."), ("B", "Have the customer continue using the current instance unchanged, since no customer data was ever accessed there."), ("C", "Escalate to Support to manually migrate the customer's data behind the scenes with no customer-facing action required."), ("D", "Have the customer downgrade to a non-GovCloud edition to avoid the instance-provisioning conflict entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The documented remediation explicitly required customer notification and a customer-driven, consent-based self-service flow (Clean Up Your Org → I Approve Data Deletion) to trigger reprovisioning into the correct instance — compliance/instance-assignment issues require explicit customer consent before data deletion and reprovisioning.\n\n* B misunderstands the compliance requirement — GovCloud Civilian customers must be on the instance designated for that specific compliance scope, regardless of whether data was accessed on the wrong one.  \n* C is not the documented resolution path; the fix explicitly required customer-facing action (review, consent, approval) via Data Cloud Setup, not a silent backend migration.  \n* D avoids the compliance requirement rather than resolving the mis-provisioning, and isn't the documented remediation."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "An org's Agentforce Observability setup depends on a Data 360 \"home connection\" that had been working for weeks before suddenly failing. The documented fix is to deactivate and reactivate the home connection, but the customer cannot perform that action themselves through the UI. What is the correct resolution path?", options: [("A", "Have the customer delete and fully reprovision Data 360 from scratch, since home connection issues require a full teardown."), ("B", "Engage Support to deactivate and reactivate the home connection, since the customer is unable to perform that specific action themselves."), ("C", "Instruct the customer to disable Agentforce Observability entirely until the home connection issue is resolved on its own."), ("D", "Have the customer create a second, parallel home connection to bypass the stuck one.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Since the fix (deactivate/reactivate the home connection) is a known, documented action but isn't accessible to the customer via self-service, engaging Support to perform it directly is the correct, proportionate resolution path.\n\n* A is a drastic overcorrection given a specific, narrow, previously-working connection issue with a known targeted fix (deactivate/reactivate).  \n* C disables functionality the customer depends on (Agentforce Observability) instead of applying the documented targeted fix.  \n* D is an unsupported workaround; a parallel home connection isn't a documented mechanism and risks configuration conflicts.\n\n*This study set now covers all 45 documented scenarios from the source canvas, one question per scenario, spanning Data Ingestion & Data Streams, Identity Resolution, Segmentation & Activation, DMO/DLO Mapping & Calculated Insights, Data Graphs, and CRM Data Streams & Permissions — plus supplementary Data Governance/Consent and Data Actions/AI items for full exam-guide section balance.*")
